  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: A cold front will pass across the lower Mid-\nAtlantic today, triggering light rain showers.\n\nTemperatures remain in the low to mid 50s areawide this morning\nahead of a cold front approaching from the west. Surface \nobservations indicate that areas of fog and drizzle have formed \nin the muggy air situated across the region, particularly across\nthe mountains. A Special Weather Statement has been issued to\nalert motorists to travel with extra caution this morning.\n\nFor today, foggy conditions will gradually improve after 8 AM,\nespecially after the cold front begins its passage across the \nregion. Not much additional warming is expected across southeast\nWest Virginia into the High Country of North Carolina, however \nthe Piedmont and foothills will have additional time to warm \ninto the 60s and possibly near 70 across Southside Virginia. The\nfront will bring light rain to portions of southeast West \nVirginia during the morning, will additional showers forming \nduring the afternoon and evening across the Piedmont. Even \ngiven the warmer afternoon temperatures expected east of the\nBlue Ridge, no thunderstorms are expected. \n\nArrival of cooler high pressure will bring dry conditions for \nTuesday under abundant sunshine and with seasonal temperatures. \nHigh pressure will pass eastward across the Great Lakes toward \nNew England, and then increasingly southward Tuesday night to \nwedge against the eastern face of the Appalachians by Wednesday\nmorning. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Wednesday and Thanksgiving Day look increasingly\ncool and wet, and mountain snow showers may arrive by Friday. \n\nAs high pressure wedges against the eastern slopes of the Blue \nRidge by Tuesday night, a complex low pressure system will \ndevelop along the Atlantic Coast of Florida on Wednesday and \ndrift northward. This low will be supported aloft by an upper \nlevel trough that will become increasingly negatively tilted as \nit passes east of the Mississippi River, which will allow it to \ndraw deep moisture from the Gulf and Atlantic coasts toward the\nMid-Atlantic. Consequently, confidence is high that Wednesday \nevening through most of Thanksgiving Day will be cool and rainy.\nThere remains some variability in the forecast concerning \ntiming and rainfall totals, but confidence is increasing that \nthe mountains will receive at least 1 inch of rain by Thursday\nnight, with the Piedmont receiving at least 1.5 inches. These \ntotals may be conservative, and will be refined as newer data \narrives. \n\nThe coastal low will depart to the northeast by Thursday night.\nA gusty northwest flow behind this low will make for increasing\ncold air advection and upslope snow showers for the mountains \nof southeast West Virginia on Friday. The northwest wind should \nsubside by Friday night. Lingering moisture from the upper level\ntrough may trigger more rain showers into the weekend, with \nsome mountain snow showers possible, but this activity appears \nweak as it will not have a favorable wind pattern with the flow \nout of the west rather than the northwest.",
